0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","4.2",{"topic":{"id":"4.2","title":"Introduction to Kali Linux Environment","minutes":12,"summary":"Kali packages security and forensic utilities; safe configuration and disciplined evidence handling determine its forensic usefulness.","sections":[{"title":"Purpose, releases, and tool selection","body":["Kali Linux is a Debian-based distribution maintained for penetration testing and security work, with forensic packages available. It is not exclusively a forensic distribution. The presence of a tool does not authorize its use, and a penetration-testing workflow may intentionally modify a target in ways unsuitable for evidence preservation.","Use official documentation and verified images for a lab environment. Record release, kernel, architecture, and package versions. Avoid running unsupported commands copied from unrelated tutorials. A current package may change defaults or output formats, so the report must identify the actual environment used."]},{"title":"Forensic mode and write protection","body":["Kali’s forensic boot options are intended to reduce automatic mounting and use of swap in supported configurations. Verify the actual boot behavior and storage configuration; a menu label alone is not proof that no writes occurred. Software precautions differ from a validated hardware write blocker, and virtualized storage introduces another layer of configuration.","Booting a suspect computer can itself change firmware state, clocks, or other evidence. Where acquisition from the running machine is inappropriate, use an approved offline method or a controlled laboratory boot environment. Document the chosen approach and its expected effects. Never assume that a live USB leaves every component untouched."]},{"title":"Isolation, privileges, and reproducibility","body":["Use synthetic data, snapshots, least privilege, and controlled networking in the classroom. Keep input evidence separate from tool output and avoid modifying the source image. Record commands or graphical settings, versions, time zone, and errors. Sensitive evidence should not be uploaded to an external service merely to make analysis easier.","Kali can support acquisition, inspection, and network analysis, but tool selection must follow the question. A password-auditing tool is not automatically appropriate for a seized device. Authority, scope, proportionality, and safety remain the examiner’s responsibility, even when a utility makes an action technically easy."]}],"practice":{"title":"Validate the boot assumptions","scenario":"A student says selecting “forensic mode” guarantees that the source disk cannot change.","steps":["List the assumptions behind that statement.","Check the documented behavior for the exact release.","Compare software precautions with a hardware write blocker.","Write a safer acquisition plan with verification and limitations."],"debrief":"Forensic mode is a configuration aid, not an unconditional guarantee. Verify what the complete environment actually does."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide"],"labId":"acquisition","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain purpose, releases, and tool selection and apply it to an investigation.","Explain forensic mode and write protection and apply it to an investigation.","Explain isolation, privileges, and reproducibility and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.2","prompt":"Does Kali forensic mode replace every other preservation safeguard?","options":["Yes, it makes all writes impossible.","No; configuration, verification, authority, and documentation still matter.","Yes, but only on laptops.","No, because Linux cannot examine evidence."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The environment must be checked in context; forensic mode is not a universal write-protection guarantee.","unit":4}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
